{
  "dom_id": "step-q335757",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A snow sensor of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor\u2019s velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the sensor is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is the weight due to gravity. Which differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the sensor is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the sensor is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =12g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the sensor, we get\n\n$$\n12g=12\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$",
